WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:05.260
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.530
Reinforcement learning is a technique where
we have a virtual creature that tries to learn

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:14.390
an optimal set of actions to maximize a reward
in a changing environment.

00:00:14.390 --> 00:00:19.829
Playing video games, helicopter control, and
even optimizing light transport simulations

00:00:19.829 --> 00:00:23.239
are among the more awesome example use cases
for it.

00:00:23.239 --> 00:00:28.220
But if we train a reinforcement learner from
scratch, we'll see that it typically starts

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:33.770
out with a brute force search in the space
of the simplest, lowest level actions.

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:39.120
This not only leads to crazy behavior early
on, but is also highly ineffective, requires

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:45.530
way more experience than humans do, and, the
obtained knowledge cannot be reused for similar

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:46.530
tasks.

00:00:46.530 --> 00:00:50.760
It can learn the game it was trained on, often
even on a superhuman level, but if we need

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.130
it to function in a new environment, all this
previous knowledge has to be thrown away.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:01:01.940
And this algorithm is very much like how humans
learn - it breaks down a big and complex task

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.519
into sequences of smaller actions.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:09.270
These are called sub-policies and can be shared
between tasks.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:14.500
Learning to walk and crawl are excellent examples
of that and will likely be reused for a variety

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:20.100
of different problems and will lead to rapid
learning on new, unseen tasks, even if they

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.060
differ significantly from the previously seen
problems.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:30.860
Not only that, but the search space over sub-policies
can easily be a hundred or more times smaller

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:36.100
than the original search space of all possible
actions, therefore this kind of search is

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.660
way more efficient than previous techniques.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:44.270
Of course, creating a good selection of sub-policies
is challenging, because they have to be robust

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:50.150
enough to be helpful on many possible tasks,
but not too specific to one problem, otherwise

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:51.440
they lose their utility.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:56.820
A few episodes ago, we mentioned a related
technique by the name Neural Task Programming,

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:03.460
and it seems that this one is capable of generalization
not only over different variations of the

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:06.900
same task, but across different tasks as well.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:12.270
These ants were trained to traverse several
different mazes one after another and quickly

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:16.500
realized that the basic movement directions
should be retained.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.410
Creating more general learning algorithms
is one of the holy grail problems of AI research,

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:25.420
and this one seems to be a proper, proper
step towards defeating it.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.840
We're not there yet, but it's hard not to
be optimistic with this incredible rate of

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:31.540
progress each year.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:35.410
Really excited to see how this area improves
over the next few months.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:38.180
The source code of this project is also available.

